Adesh, since we’re going with the "Real Engineering" approach—keeping it simple but powerful—here is the JPQL query for your TasksRepository.
This single query handles five different filters at once. If a parameter is passed, it filters; if it’s null, it ignores that filter. This is the cleanest way to build a "Search & Filter" feature without adding the complexity of Elasticsearch.
🏛️ The Repository Implementation
Add this to your TasksRepository.java. We use the IS NULL trick, which is a standard pattern in professional SQL to make parameters optional.
@Repository
public interface TasksRepository extends JpaRepository<Tasks, Long> {

    @Query("SELECT t FROM Tasks t WHERE " +
           "(:projectId IS NULL OR t.project.id = :projectId) AND " +
           "(:status IS NULL OR t.status = :status) AND " +
           "(:priority IS NULL OR t.priority = :priority) AND " +
           "(:assigneeId IS NULL OR t.assignee.id = :assigneeId) AND " +
           "(:searchText IS NULL OR (LOWER(t.title) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%', :searchText, '%')) OR " +
           "LOWER(t.description) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%', :searchText, '%'))))")
    List<Tasks> searchTasks(
            @Param("projectId") Long projectId,
            @Param("status") TaskStatus status,
            @Param("priority") TaskPriority priority,
            @Param("assigneeId") Long assigneeId,
            @Param("searchText") String searchText
    );
}

🔍 Why this is "Senior Level" Logic
 * The IS NULL OR Logic: This is the magic part. If you don't send a priority from the frontend, the first half of the OR is true, so the database skips that filter entirely.
 * Case Insensitivity: By using LOWER(t.title) and LOWER(:searchText), your search will find "JWT" even if the user types "jwt".
 * The CONCAT Wildcard: Using '%', :searchText, '%' allows for a "Contains" search. It will find "Fix JWT bug" if the user just types "JWT".
 * One Round Trip: Instead of fetching all tasks and filtering them in Java (which is slow), you are letting the Database Engine do the hard work. This is much faster.
🛠️ How to use it in your Service
In your TaskService, you can now just pass the parameters straight from your Controller.
public List<TaskResDto> getAllTasks(Long projectId, TaskStatus status, TaskPriority priority, Long assigneeId, String search) {
    // Call the dynamic repository query
    List<Tasks> taskList = tasksRepository.searchTasks(projectId, status, priority, assigneeId, search);
    
    // Map to DTO and return
    return taskList.stream()
            .map(this::convertToDto)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
}

